 "issue": 1419,
 "issue_title": "Bug: quote numeric-looking strings in schema_to_yaml",
 "issue_body": "## Summary\n\n`schema_to_yaml()` emits numeric-looking strings without quotes. Values such as `\"001\"`, `\"123\"`, or `\"1.5\"` can be parsed as numbers by downstream YAML parsers, losing the original string type and formatting.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nSchema exports must preserve contract values exactly. Allowed string values often include codes, ZIP/postal codes, IDs, or category labels where leading zeros are meaningful.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `arnio/schema_export.py::_emit_scalar()` quotes booleans/null-like strings and strings with special characters.\n- It does not quote strings that look like integers or floats.\n- Existing YAML tests cover booleans, null-like words, empty strings, colons, and newlines, but not numeric-looking strings.\n\n## Reproduction or trigger\n\n```python\nfrom arnio.schema_export import schema_to_yaml\n\nprint(schema_to_yaml({\n    \"status\": {\n        \"type\": \"STRING\",\n        \"allowed\": [\"001\", \"123\", \"1.5\"],\n    }\n}))\n```\n\nCurrent output:\n\n```yaml\nfields:\n  status:\n    allowed:\n      - 001\n      - 123\n      - 1.5\n    type: STRING\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nString values that could be interpreted as numbers should be quoted:\n\n```yaml\nallowed:\n  - \"001\"\n  - \"123\"\n  - \"1.5\"\n```\n\n## Actual behavior\n\nThey are emitted bare and may be loaded back as numeric values by YAML tooling.\n\n## Suggested direction\n\n- Update `_emit_scalar()` to quote numeric-looking strings.\n- Include integers, floats, signed numbers, and strings with leading zeros in tests.\n- Keep actual `int` and `float` values emitted as numbers.\n\n## References\n\n- `arnio/schema_export.py`\n- `tests/test_schema_export.py`\r\n",
 "issue_url": "https://github.com/im-anishraj/arnio/issues/1419",
 "pr": 1554,
 "pr_title": "fix: quote numeric-looking strings in schema_to_yaml ",
 "pr_body": "## Summary\r\nUpdated `_emit_scalar()` in `arnio/schema_export.py` to identify and wrap numeric-looking strings (such as `\"001\"`, `\"123\"`, and `\"1.5\"`) in double quotes. This ensures schema exports preserve string semantics exactly and prevents downstream YAML parsers from misinterpreting string variables as numbers. Real integers and floats pass through untouched.\r\n\r\n## Linked issue\r\nFixes #1419\r\n\r\n## Type of change\r\n- [x] Bug fix\r\n- [ ] Feature\r\n- [ ] Performance improvement\r\n- [ ] Documentation\r\n- [x] Tests\r\n- [ ] Refactor\r\n- [ ] CI / packaging\r\n\r\n## Area\r\n- [ ] CSV parser / I/O\r\n- [ ] C++ cleaning engine\r\n- [ ] Python API / ArFrame\r\n- [ ] Pipeline / custom steps\r\n- [ ] Data quality / profiling\r\n- [x] Schema validation\r\n- [ ] pandas interoperability\r\n- [ ] Docs / website\r\n- [ ] Developer tooling\r\n\r\n## Testing\r\nRan the comprehensive local validation suite. All existing and new test cases passed successfully.\r\n- [x] `make test`: 1982 passed\r\n- [x] `make lint`\r\n- [ ] Other:\r\n\r\n## Screenshots / Media\r\nN/A\r\n\r\n## Performance Impact\r\nNone\r\n\r\n## GSSoC labels\r\n- level:beginner\r\n- type:bug\r\n- area:schema\r\n\r\n## Contributor checklist\r\n- [x] I read the contributing guide.\r\n- [x] I kept the PR focused on one issue or one logical change.\r\n- [x] I added or updated tests for behavior changes.\r\n- [x] I added or updated docs/examples for public API changes.\r\n- [x] I checked that generated files, logs, and local build artifacts are not committed.\r\n- [x] My PR title uses Conventional Commits (`fix: quote numeric-looking strings in schema_to_yaml`).\r\n\r\n## Maintainer notes",
